<Base
	title="Engineers in charge"
	description="A manifesto for engineers working with AI agents: what changed, what doesn’t, and how to do it well."
>
	<article class="wrap">
		<header>
			<h1>Engineers in charge</h1>
			<p class="lede">A manifesto for engineers working with AI agents.</p>
		</header>
		<div class="prose">
			<p>
				This is for engineers who want what AI makes possible without handing over the wheel. It’s what I’ve come
				to believe after months of getting it wrong, and it’s what I’m building toward.
			</p>

			<h2>What changed</h2>
			<p>
				<strong>AI has caused a temporal shift.</strong> Work that used to take a day or two now takes under an
				hour. The work didn’t get easier; the slow parts did. Reading docs for a system you’ve never touched,
				hunting for the right setting, writing the boilerplate.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Context switching should be nearly free.</strong> You already know how these systems work. What
				wears you down is relearning every detail just to finish one task. An agent can carry that detail for you.
				You still need to understand how the system works. You don’t need to memorize every vendor’s knobs.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Less grind, more of the work that matters.</strong> Hand the tedious parts to the agent and spend your
				time on design, judgment and the problems only you can solve.
			</p>

			<h2>What doesn’t change</h2>
			<p>
				<strong>Models are great at assumptions. Assumptions aren’t decisions.</strong> An agent will happily fill
				every gap with a confident guess. Context, steering, correction and refinement are the job now, and that’s
				why engineers aren’t being replaced.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>You own what ships, no matter who typed it.</strong> If an agent wrote the change, it still goes out
				under your name.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Every agent decision is on the record.</strong> Even the small break-fix calls an agent makes on its
				own get logged and can be reviewed. That doesn’t mean a person approves every step. Review scales with
				risk: small fixes are recorded, risky changes wait for a human.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>An agent is just another user of your platform.</strong> It gets the same well-paved paths and the
				same firm boundaries as everyone else. Give engineers room to move fast, and put the limits where they
				protect everyone.
			</p>

			<h2>How to do it well</h2>
			<p>
				<strong>Fit AI to your workflow, not your workflow to AI.</strong> Same tools, same reviews, same on-call.
				The pace of the work changes, not its shape. It’s a smaller shift than most teams expect.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Your tools should bend to you.</strong> The best engineers I know are all opinionated about their
				tools, and they’re right to be. Agent tools should be customizable to how you work, stable enough to trust,
				and never tied to a single model.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Doing it safely is the hard part.</strong> Speed is easy to demo. Guardrails, audit trails and
				sensible defaults are what make it last. That’s the part I care most about, and it’s the part I’m building.
			</p>

			<p class="closing">Let the agent drive the work. Keep your hands on the wheel.</p>
		</div>
	</article>
</Base>

